research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1133806-+{"id":"lem-higher-order-rolle","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.clas.ufl.edu/bruceedwards/files/GeneralRolle.pdf"],"rationale":"The source states the same higher-order Rolle theorem: continuity on the closed interval, n differentiability in the interior, n+1 ordered zeros, and a zero of the nth derivative between the extreme zeros. The numbered iterative proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1133807-+{"id":"thm-darboux-theorem-for-derivatives","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_mvt.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"Both sources state that the derivative of a differentiable real function on an interval has the intermediate value property, with the same quantifiers and direction. The extremum-based numbered proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1133808-+{"id":"cor-derivatives-have-no-removable-or-jump-discontinuities","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf","https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~ancoop/3600/section-25.html"],"rationale":"The sources explain that a derivative has Darboux's property and hence cannot have a jump, while giving the standard oscillatory derivative discontinuity. The item translates this into the library's removable/jump/essential classification; the removable case is the same local intermediate-value obstruction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
