research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348047-+{"id":"lem-higher-order-rolle","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.clas.ufl.edu/bruceedwards/files/GeneralRolle.pdf"],"rationale":"The source states the same higher-order Rolle theorem: continuity on the closed interval, n differentiability in the interior, n+1 ordered zeros, and a zero of the nth derivative between the extreme zeros. The numbered iterative proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348048-+{"id":"thm-darboux-theorem-for-derivatives","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_mvt.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"Both sources state that the derivative of a differentiable real function on an interval has the intermediate value property, with the same quantifiers and direction. The extremum-based numbered proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348049-+{"id":"cor-derivatives-have-no-removable-or-jump-discontinuities","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf","https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~ancoop/3600/section-25.html"],"rationale":"The sources explain that a derivative has Darboux's property and hence cannot have a jump, while giving the standard oscillatory derivative discontinuity. The item translates this into the library's removable/jump/essential classification; the removable case is the same local intermediate-value obstruction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348050-+{"id":"lem-injective-darboux-function-is-strictly-monotone","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://doiserbia.nb.rs/Article.aspx?id=1452-86302100024M"],"rationale":"The peer-reviewed source states the same theorem that an injective real function on an interval with the Darboux property is strictly monotone. The repository's order-trichotomy proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1348051:+{"id":"cor-injective-or-monotone-derivative-is-continuous","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://doiserbia.nb.rs/Article.aspx?id=1452-86302100024M","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_mvt.html"],"rationale":"The literature supplies the two standard ingredients: derivatives have the Darboux property, and injective Darboux functions are strictly monotone. The item packages those with the standard continuity theorem for monotone functions having interval image, and adds the already-monotone alternative.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348052-+{"id":"lem-cauchy-mean-value-quotient-form","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_mvt.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state Cauchy's mean value theorem in quotient form under continuity, differentiability, and nonvanishing g', including the necessary conclusion g(b) not equal to g(a). The short derivation is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348053-+{"id":"thm-lhopital-zero-over-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~kouba/CalcOneDIRECTORY/lhopitaldirectory/LHopital.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the 0/0 rule under the same derivative-quotient and nonvanishing-denominator-derivative hypotheses. The item uniformly packages one-sided, two-sided, infinite-endpoint, and extended-real limits and derives infinity by t=1/x, so it is a convention-rich semantic restatement rather than an exact copy.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348054-+{"id":"thm-lhopital-infinity-over-infinity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~kouba/CalcOneDIRECTORY/lhopitaldirectory/LHopital.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the infinity-over-infinity rule for finite and infinite endpoints. The item consolidates endpoint modes, extended-real derivative limits, absolute divergence, and eventual fixed signs in the library's conventions. Its Cauchy-mean-value proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
