research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1448355-+{"id":"lem-higher-order-rolle","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.clas.ufl.edu/bruceedwards/files/GeneralRolle.pdf"],"rationale":"The source states the same higher-order Rolle theorem: continuity on the closed interval, n differentiability in the interior, n+1 ordered zeros, and a zero of the nth derivative between the extreme zeros. The numbered iterative proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1448356-+{"id":"thm-darboux-theorem-for-derivatives","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_mvt.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"Both sources state that the derivative of a differentiable real function on an interval has the intermediate value property, with the same quantifiers and direction. The extremum-based numbered proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1448357-+{"id":"cor-derivatives-have-no-removable-or-jump-discontinuities","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf","https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~ancoop/3600/section-25.html"],"rationale":"The sources explain that a derivative has Darboux's property and hence cannot have a jump, while giving the standard oscillatory derivative discontinuity. The item translates this into the library's removable/jump/essential classification; the removable case is the same local intermediate-value obstruction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1448358-+{"id":"lem-injective-darboux-function-is-strictly-monotone","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://doiserbia.nb.rs/Article.aspx?id=1452-86302100024M"],"rationale":"The peer-reviewed source states the same theorem that an injective real function on an interval with the Darboux property is strictly monotone. The repository's order-trichotomy proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1448359:+{"id":"cor-injective-or-monotone-derivative-is-continuous","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://doiserbia.nb.rs/Article.aspx?id=1452-86302100024M","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_mvt.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_monotonefunc.html"],"rationale":"The literature supplies the three exact ingredients: derivatives have the Darboux property, injective Darboux functions are strictly monotone, and a monotone function on an interval with interval image is continuous. The item packages them into the injective-or-monotone derivative corollary.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1448360-+{"id":"lem-cauchy-mean-value-quotient-form","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_mvt.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state Cauchy's mean value theorem in quotient form under continuity, differentiability, and nonvanishing g', including the necessary conclusion g(b) not equal to g(a). The short derivation is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1448361-+{"id":"thm-lhopital-zero-over-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~kouba/CalcOneDIRECTORY/lhopitaldirectory/LHopital.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the 0/0 rule under the same derivative-quotient and nonvanishing-denominator-derivative hypotheses. The item uniformly packages one-sided, two-sided, infinite-endpoint, and extended-real limits and derives infinity by t=1/x, so it is a convention-rich semantic restatement rather than an exact copy.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
